---
import Dashboard from "../layouts/Dashboard.astro"
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro"
import { Image } from "astro:assets"
import { getEntry } from "astro:content"
import { route } from "../lib/route"

const summary = await getEntry("analysis", "summary")
const reportContext = await getEntry("reportContext", "context")
const projectName = reportContext?.data.title ?? summary?.data.metadata.name ?? "Planetnik"
---

<Dashboard title="Technology" projectName={projectName}>
  <div class="tech">
    <Hero tagline="The instruments behind the forecast" />

    <p class="lead">
      How Płanetnik is built, what it runs on, and why that matters for teams adopting it.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Agentic Architecture Modernisation (AAM)</h3>

    <p>
      Płanetnik is an experiment in a different way of building software.
    </p>

    <p>
      The majority of the codebase was written by AI coding agents — primarily
      <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code</a> and
      <a href="https://openai.com/index/introducing-codex/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Codex</a> — working under human
      architectural direction. The human role was not to write most of the code, but to set constraints,
      review output, and steer decisions. The agents did the implementation.
    </p>

    <p>
      This only works when the guardrails are strong enough to keep the agents productive without drifting.
      Three practices made this possible:
    </p>

    <dl>
      <dt>Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)</dt>
      <dd>
        Every significant technical choice — the Effect stack, the concurrency model, the content collection
        architecture, the landscape analysis pipeline — is captured in a written ADR before implementation begins.
        Agents receive ADRs as context and are expected to follow them — and, critically, they are observed
        to push back when implementation conflicts with a recorded decision. This prevents architectural drift
        across sessions and agents, gives the human a durable record of <em>why</em> decisions were made, and
        turns the ADR corpus into a living constraint system rather than passive documentation.
      </dd>

      <dt>Domain-Driven Design (DDD)</dt>
      <dd>
        The domain model — repositories, components, contributors, coupling, hotspots, impact — is explicit and
        shared across the codebase. Agents work within named domain boundaries rather than inventing ad-hoc
        abstractions. This keeps the code navigable and the vocabulary consistent, even when different agents
        contribute to different parts of the system.
      </dd>

      <dt>Test-Driven Development (TDD)</dt>
      <dd>
        Tests are written before implementation. Agents are expected to make tests pass, not just produce code
        that compiles. This gives a fast, objective feedback loop: either the behaviour is correct or it is not.
        TDD also acts as a specification language that agents can follow without ambiguity.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      The result is a codebase that was largely agent-written but remains structurally coherent, well-tested,
      and architecturally intentional. The practices that make this work are not specific to AI — they are the
      same practices that keep large human teams aligned. The difference is that with agents, the cost of
      skipping them is faster and more visible.
    </p>

    <p>
      Płanetnik treats this as a practical question, not a philosophical one: can you modernise a mature Java
      tool into a TypeScript stack at speed, using AI agents as the primary implementers, without losing
      architectural control? The answer so far is yes — if the engineering discipline is already in place.
    </p>

    <div class="section-banner">
      <Image
        src={route("/plantnik-banner-conduct.webp")}
        alt="Agentic architecture modernisation (AAM)"
        width={1536}
        height={1024}
        class="section-banner-image"
      />
    </div>

    <hr />

    <h3>Why Not Just Run Sokrates?</h3>

    <p>
      Płanetnik is inspired by <a href="https://www.sokrates.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sokrates</a>, the pragmatic source code examination
      tool created by Željko Obrenović. Sokrates is excellent, but running it in practice introduces operational overhead
      that can slow adoption.
    </p>
    <hr />
    <h3>Płanetnik's Approach</h3>
    <p>
      Płanetnik replaces the Java runtime with a single <strong>Bun</strong> binary and a TypeScript codebase.
      The entire tool — CLI, analysis engine, and report site — runs from source with no compile step, no JVM, no
      Graphviz, and no Docker requirement.
    </p>

    <div class="comparison">
      <div class="comparison-card">
        <h4>Sokrates</h4>
        <dl>
          <dt>Runtime</dt>
          <dd>JDK 11+ (150-300 MB)</dd>
          <dt>Diagrams</dt>
          <dd>Graphviz native binary</dd>
          <dt>CI setup</dt>
          <dd>Java + Graphviz + JAR, or Docker image</dd>
          <dt>Config format</dt>
          <dd>JSON with system environment variables</dd>
          <dt>Reports</dt>
          <dd>Static HTML generated by Java</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="comparison-card highlight">
        <h4>Płanetnik</h4>
        <dl>
          <dt>Runtime</dt>
          <dd>Bun (~30 MB single binary)</dd>
          <dt>Diagrams</dt>
          <dd>Browser-rendered (React, visx, Sigma.js)</dd>
          <dt>CI setup</dt>
          <dd>Bun install + run from source</dd>
          <dt>Config format</dt>
          <dd>TypeScript config with Effect schema validation</dd>
          <dt>Reports</dt>
          <dd>Astro static site with interactive React components</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The result is that a full analysis — clone, scan, build reports, deploy — can run in a single GitHub Actions
      job with no container, no pre-built image, and no native dependency management.
    </p>

    <div class="benchmark">
      <div class="benchmark-value">53 seconds</div>
      <div class="benchmark-label">
        Landscape analysis of the <a href="https://github.com/effect-TS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Effect-TS</a> organisation — 17 repositories
        cloned, scanned, and aggregated with concurrency 4 on a standard GitHub Actions runner.
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr />

    <h3>Beyond Sokrates</h3>

    <p>
      Płanetnik is not just a port. It extends the original Sokrates model with capabilities that go beyond
      what the Java tool provides:
    </p>

    <dl>
      <dt>Balanced Coupling</dt>
      <dd>
        Płanetnik implements the <a href="https://coupling.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Balanced Coupling</a> model by
        <a href="https://vladikk.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vlad Khononov</a>, which blends static dependencies, temporal co-change,
        coordination overlap, and knowledge exposure into a single coupling pressure view. The accompanying
        Big Ball of Mud visualisation renders the coupling topology as an interactive force-directed graph,
        making it possible to see at a glance which components are overloaded and where architectural boundaries
        are breaking down.
      </dd>

      <dt>Impact Analysis</dt>
      <dd>
        A structural impact model that separates visible activity (commits, files changed) from deeper
        architectural impact (complexity reduction, boundary shaping, knowledge spread, coordination relief,
        change enablement). This helps teams distinguish high-leverage work from surface-level churn.
      </dd>

      <dt>Hotspot Prioritisation</dt>
      <dd>
        A multi-dimensional scoring model that ranks files, units, and components by structural risk, change
        pressure, coordination cost, and knowledge risk — producing a prioritised queue of what to fix next
        rather than a flat list of metrics.
      </dd>

      <dt>Landscape Mode</dt>
      <dd>
        Multi-repository analysis across entire GitHub organisations, with cross-repo contributor tracking,
        team topology, tag-based grouping, and per-repository drill-down — all from a single build.
      </dd>

      <dt>Interactive Reports</dt>
      <dd>
        Every chart is a browser-rendered React component — zoomable, filterable, and theme-aware.
        No static PNGs, no Graphviz dependency, no server-side rendering.
      </dd>

      <dt>Applied Usability Heuristics</dt>
      <dd>
        The report interface is designed around
        <a href="https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nielsen's ten usability heuristics</a>,
        not just technical capability. System status is always visible — loading states, empty states, and data
        freshness are explicit rather than ambiguous. Navigation follows real-world conventions with consistent
        terminology across pages. Users can search globally with instant fuzzy matching, recover from wrong turns
        via persistent breadcrumbs, and switch between light and dark themes without losing context. Layouts are
        fully responsive — the same report reads well on a widescreen monitor during a review and on a phone
        during an incident. Error states are descriptive, not cryptic. Where Sokrates generates functional but
        utilitarian HTML, Płanetnik treats the report surface as a product: consistent visual hierarchy,
        keyboard-accessible controls, and interface patterns that reduce cognitive load rather than add to it.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <hr />

    <h3>Core Stack</h3>

    <h4>Bun</h4>
    <p>
      <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bun</a> is the runtime, package manager, test runner, and bundler.
      It replaces Node.js, npm, Jest, and esbuild with a single binary. Płanetnik runs directly from
      <code>.ts</code> source files — no transpilation step, no <code>dist/</code> folder in development.
      In CI, <code>bun run apps/cli/src/index.ts</code> executes the CLI directly from source.
    </p>

    <h4>TypeScript</h4>
    <p>
      Strict mode with <code>exactOptionalPropertyTypes</code>. The entire codebase — analysis engine, CLI,
      report site — is TypeScript. Type safety is enforced at every boundary: configuration schemas, content
      collections, component props, and CLI arguments.
    </p>

    <h4>Effect</h4>
    <p>
      <a href="https://effect.website" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Effect</a> provides the service architecture, error handling, concurrency model,
      and dependency injection. The CLI uses <code>@effect/cli</code> for command parsing. Configuration and data schemas
      use <code>@effect/schema</code> instead of manual JSON validation. File I/O uses <code>@effect/platform</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For landscape analysis — where dozens of repositories are cloned, scanned, and aggregated in parallel — Effect's
      <strong>structured concurrency</strong> is essential. Each repository analysis runs as a scoped effect with
      deterministic resource cleanup: temporary clone directories are acquired within Effect scope and released by
      finalizers, not by fragile <code>try/finally</code> blocks. Bounded concurrency is expressed declaratively rather
      than through manual array batching, and interruption propagates cleanly — a cancelled run does not leave orphaned
      temp directories or dangling network connections. Retry policies for flaky operations like git clones and GitHub
      API calls are explicit and bounded rather than implicit or absent.
    </p>
    <p>
      This matters most at scale. When a landscape run processes 17 repositories concurrently, the difference between
      structured and ad-hoc concurrency is the difference between reliable cleanup and resource leaks, between clear
      failure reporting and silently swallowed errors.
    </p>

    <h4>Astro</h4>
    <p>
      <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a> generates the report site as a fully static build. Pages are
      server-rendered at build time from content collections populated by the analysis output. The result is a
      zero-JavaScript-by-default site that loads fast and can be deployed anywhere static files are served —
      GitHub Pages, S3, Netlify, or a local file server.
    </p>

    <h4>React + Data Visualisation</h4>
    <p>
      Interactive charts and tables use React islands hydrated by Astro. The visualisation stack includes:
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt><a href="https://airbnb.io/visx/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">visx</a></dt>
      <dd>Low-level composable primitives for bar charts, heatmaps, treemaps, axes, and scales — built on D3 without the D3 abstraction tax</dd>
      <dt><a href="https://www.sigmajs.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sigma.js</a> + <a href="https://graphology.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Graphology</a></dt>
      <dd>WebGL graph rendering for dependency networks, team topology, and coupling visualisations</dd>
      <dt><a href="https://reactflow.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">React Flow</a></dt>
      <dd>Node-based diagrams for component dependency graphs</dd>
      <dt><a href="https://tanstack.com/table" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TanStack Table</a></dt>
      <dd>Headless table engine powering sortable, filterable data grids across all report pages</dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      All charts render in the browser — no server-side image generation, no Graphviz, no SVG export pipeline.
      This means diagrams are interactive, zoomable, and theme-aware by default.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Architecture</h3>

    <p>Płanetnik is a monorepo with clear package boundaries:</p>

    <dl>
      <dt><code>packages/analyzer</code></dt>
      <dd>Core analysis engine — file scanning, git history, duplication detection, complexity, coupling, hotspots, and impact scoring</dd>
      <dt><code>packages/common</code></dt>
      <dd>Shared types, JSON I/O, rendering utilities, and colour palettes</dd>
      <dt><code>packages/reports</code></dt>
      <dd>HTML report generation, chart utilities, and data exporters</dd>
      <dt><code>apps/cli</code></dt>
      <dd>Effect CLI application — <code>analyzeLocal</code>, <code>analyzeRemote</code>, <code>analyzeLandscape</code>, and supporting commands</dd>
      <dt><code>apps/site</code></dt>
      <dd>Astro report site — reads analysis output as content collections and renders the full interactive dashboard</dd>
    </dl>

    <hr />

    <h3>Operational Model</h3>

    <h4>Single-Repository Analysis</h4>
    <p>
      Clone a repository, run the CLI, build the site. The entire pipeline is a single CI job:
      <code>bun install</code> → <code>bun run analyzeRemote</code> → <code>bun run build</code> → deploy static files.
    </p>

    <h4>Landscape Analysis</h4>
    <p>
      Provide a JSON config listing GitHub organisations or repositories. The CLI clones and analyses each repository
      in parallel (configurable concurrency), aggregates contributors, teams, and tags across the landscape, and produces
      a unified dataset. The same Astro site renders landscape-level and per-repository views from a single build.
    </p>

    <h4>No Docker Required</h4>
    <p>
      Because the only runtime dependency is Bun, there is no need for a custom Docker image. CI runners with
      Node.js and Bun installed can run the full pipeline directly. This removes image build and registry
      management from the operational surface.
    </p>

    <h4>Deploy Anywhere</h4>
    <p>
      The output is a static site. It can be served from GitHub Pages, any CDN, an internal file server, or
      opened locally from disk. There is no application server, no database, and no ongoing runtime cost.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Design Decisions</h3>

    <p>
      Many of the choices below follow the distinction Rich Hickey draws in
      <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SxdOUGdseq4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Simple Made Easy</em></a>:
      prefer simple over easy, separate what from how, and avoid complecting things that can stay apart.
    </p>

    <dl>
      <dt>Port first, refine second</dt>
      <dd>
        The initial migration ported Java classes and business logic 1:1 into TypeScript classes — not idiomatic
        TypeScript, but <em>simple</em>. Matching the original structure let the LLM agents focus on tests and
        straightforward porting rather than simultaneous redesign. Once the test suite was green and behaviour
        was verified, a second pass reshaped the code toward idiomatic Effect, functional patterns, and cleaner
        TypeScript. Two simple steps instead of one <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SxdOUGdseq4&t=822s" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">complected</a> leap.
      </dd>

      <dt>Run from source, not from a JAR</dt>
      <dd>Bun's native TypeScript execution means there is no build artifact to manage. The source <em>is</em> the program.</dd>

      <dt>Browser rendering, not server-side image generation</dt>
      <dd>Charts are React components, not PNGs. They respond to theme changes, resize with the viewport, and support interaction.</dd>

      <dt>Content collections, not template strings</dt>
      <dd>Analysis output is loaded as typed Astro content collections with Zod schemas. The site has compile-time guarantees that data shapes are correct.</dd>

      <dt>Effect services, not ad-hoc dependency wiring</dt>
      <dd>The analysis engine uses Effect layers for dependency injection, making it testable and composable without global state.</dd>

      <dt>Static output, not a running application</dt>
      <dd>Reports are pre-rendered HTML. Once built, they need no server, no API, and no authentication. They can be archived, diffed, and version-controlled.</dd>
    </dl>

  </div>
</Dashboard>

